    <h1>Chapter One</h1>
    <div class="chapter-body">
      <div class="epigraph"><em>The world has built machines that count every man who crosses it, and the house has learned to read them. The watch is easier now than it has ever been. My predecessor wrote that ease is how a charge dies.</em><br />— The Annals of the Watch, 389th generation</div>
            <hr />
            <p>The trouble started at a security checkpoint — not the kind he was used to.</p>
            <p>They landed that afternoon in a Gulf capital, on a private apron of white concrete shimmering with heat. The owner was taking aboard a royal guest, and the royal traveled with a protection detail that did not ask a country's permission so much as inform it. Before the crew could touch the airplane, every one of them had to clear the visitors' security: a walk-through gate, hand wands, and a baggage X-ray, set up in a cordoned lane on the ramp and run by unsmiling men in good suits.</p>
            <p>Roan beeped.</p>
            <p>He wasn't carrying anything. Roan knew exactly what was on his body at all times — belt, watch, phone, two old challenge coins, the shoes. Nothing that should trip a magnetometer.</p>
            <p>"Again, please." The head of the detail had a low, final voice.</p>
            <p>Back, and through. <em>Beep.</em></p>
            <p>Belt off. Through. <em>Beep.</em> Watch off. <em>Beep.</em> Two more suits drifted over to watch. "Empty the pockets. Plates, pins, implants."</p>
            <p>"No plates. No pins. No implants." His pockets were already in a tray. He held his arms out from his sides. "For what it's worth, I'm on your side here."</p>
            <p>"The shoes."</p>
            <p>Shoes off. Through in his socks. <em>Beep.</em></p>
            <p>Four. Roan had a rule about four of anything: four was where a pattern stopped being noise and became a decision. The owner's cabin was open, the clock was running, and he and this magnetometer were not going to resolve their differences. So he changed instruments. At the end of the lane, the crew's bags were riding a belt through a proper X-ray machine.</p>
            <p>He walked to the head of the belt, set a shoe on the rollers, and climbed on.</p>
            <p>A grown man does not pass cleanly through a baggage scanner, but with sufficient commitment he passes. The lead flaps dragged over his shoulders. Somewhere a screener got a once-in-a-career look at a live rib cage. Roan rode the belt out the far side, sat up among the trays under the appalled stares of a foreign king's bodyguards, and spread his arms.</p>
            <p>"Are we okay now?"</p>
            <p>Two of the suits had hands inside their jackets. Then, from the top of the airstairs, someone began — helplessly — to laugh.</p>
            <p>They pulled him off the belt and wanded him one last time, by hand, slowly. The wand said nothing. Not a chirp. Clean — which made the entire ceremony retroactively insane. Roan noted it and filed it where he'd been filing things lately. <em>The walk-through beeps. The wand doesn't. That is not how machines work.</em> But a king was coming up the stairs and there was an airplane to fly, so he did what he did with everything that didn't fit: put it in the drawer, shut the drawer, and went to work.</p>
            <hr />
            <p>The job was the best in the world, and Roan had arranged his life so that he never had to explain it.</p>
            <p>He was captain of a private 787 — a wide-body built to carry two hundred and fifty strangers, gutted and rebuilt to carry one man and whoever that man wanted near him. On paper the airplane belonged to a holding company. The holding company belonged to a name Roan had met exactly twice in three years: a reclusive, staggeringly rich old man who traveled constantly and was never photographed. The flight department paid like a sovereign fund and asked only that its pilots be perfect, discreet, and available. Roan was all three.</p>
            <p>The money had made the question of money disappear from his life. What the job really gave him was motion. Weeks off at a stretch, then a summons, a tail number, and a flight plan to somewhere he'd have to look up. The job had taken him into airfields most airline pilots never see, and out of a couple that weren't technically airfields at all. He kept one bag packed for the tropics and one for the ice. Between trips there was time — huge, unstructured time — and he spent it going down holes in the earth, off the tops of cliffs and towers, and into water nobody sane would enter, in the private pursuit of the only quiet he'd ever found.</p>
            <p>That week he was carrying the tropics bag. The airplane was going somewhere warm, and after that, somewhere he'd been quietly steering himself toward for a month. He told himself the two were unrelated.</p>
            <p>About what mattered most, he was a liar — mostly to himself.</p>
            <hr />
            <p>New York, that morning. The model's name was Lena, and she pretended to be asleep when Roan left, and he pretended to believe her. It was the kindest either of them had been in six weeks.</p>
            <p>Six weeks was the number. It always was. He would have told you it was discipline — brief, clean, ended before anyone said a word they'd have to pretend to mean — and for years he had believed his own briefing. The truth was stranger, and he had stopped examining it: somewhere around week five, something in him consulted a calendar he had never seen and declared the arrangement complete. It wasn't cruelty. It wasn't even a decision. It had never once explained itself, and he had learned not to ask. Lena was lovely, and had asked nothing of him, and around week five she'd started leaving things — a toothbrush, a hair tie, a certain look across restaurant tables — and the calendar, wherever it was kept, had already ruled. He'd booked the trip that would end it. He usually had.</p>
            <p>She surfaced just far enough to watch him dress and not far enough to let him know it. He stood at the window pulling his shirt on, Manhattan a flat dawn gray behind him, the towers still holding their last few lit windows. Deep-brown eyes that in exactly this light — and only ever in exactly this light — showed a scatter of green buried far down in them, there and gone. A face of heavy bone and hard classical lines. She'd told him once that he looked like a statue. He'd said the Romans had famously terrible teeth, and changed the subject. Much later, she would decide it was the most honest answer he ever gave her.</p>
            <p>She knew almost nothing about him. A Navy something he wouldn't discuss. A job that took him to the ends of the earth on no notice. A body with no tattoos and no scars worth the name — she'd noticed, because in her experience men like that always carried some history on their skin — except one small mark on the inside of his right forearm, a dark open curve no bigger than a thumbnail. She'd traced it once while he slept. It looked less like a birthmark than like a letter, a character from an alphabet she couldn't place. He'd never mentioned it. She had the feeling he no longer saw it at all.</p>
            <p>What Lena didn't know was that the locked door was not a strategy. Women wanted him because the distance read like a door with something behind it. There was no room behind the door. Roan was capable of two kinds of love. One was for a machine that would kill him if he stopped paying attention. The other was for the short list of friends who had once carried him out of places nobody was supposed to walk out of — two or three men, scattered around the world, for whom he would cross any ocean, cancel any plan, and lie to any judge. He never mentioned them. Mentioning it was how you got asked for more.</p>
            <p>He left a note that said <em>Coffee's programmed for 9. — R,</em> rode the elevator down forty floors, and went to work.</p>
            <hr />
            <p>In the cockpit, on the far side of the scanner ceremony, Roan was the calmest and most complete version of himself that existed. Everyone who flew with him learned it inside an hour.</p>
            <p>The flight deck was the one room he'd ever found where the world agreed to be a system. Numbers meant what they said. Procedures ran the same way every time. The wiring that ended dinner parties early — the endless tallying, the patterns, the ability to hold a hundred moving variables in his head at once and feel the instant one of them drifted — up here, that wiring was simply the reason he was one of the best pilots most people would ever fly with. Doctors had explained it to him a long time ago: a high-functioning corner of a particular spectrum. His height was a fact; so was this. It had never once let him down.</p>
            <p>He came up the airstairs two minutes behind schedule, in his socks, shoes in one fist, and slid into the left seat. The flight deck was cool and dim after the white glare of the ramp.</p>
            <p>The first officer had watched all of it from the flight deck windows. His name was Otto. He was twenty-six, built like a fencepost with excellent posture, uniform pressed to a parade standard. In the eleven months since Roan had hired him, he had said perhaps four hundred words, every one of them load-bearing. He turned, took Roan in — the socks, the shoes, the hair — and held up his phone, screen out. On it, in security-camera gray, a man in a captain's uniform rode a baggage belt into an X-ray machine.</p>
            <p>"Already?" Roan said.</p>
            <p>"Group chat," Otto said, and put the phone away.</p>
            <p>Roan pulled the checklist toward him and felt what only ever happened up here begin to happen: all the moving pieces settling into place, every one exactly where it belonged.</p>
            <p>"Before-start," he said.</p>
            <p>For Roan, an atheist to the bone, that was about as close to a prayer as it got.</p>    </div>
